--- !u!114 &1582174451
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1582174449}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The human respiratory system is the organ system responsible for breathing.
    Its main function is to bring oxygen into the body and remove carbon dioxide,
    a waste gas produced by cells. The major parts of the respiratory system include
    the nose, trachea, bronchi, lungs, and diaphragm.

    When we inhale, air enters
    through the nose. It then travels down the trachea, also known as the windpipe.
    The trachea divides into two tubes called the bronchi, with one bronchus leading
    into each lung. Inside the lungs, the bronchi divide into smaller air passages
    that carry air deeper into the lungs.

    At the ends of these small passages
    are millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli. This is where gas exchange takes
    place. Oxygen from the inhaled air passes through the thin walls of the alveoli
    and enters the blood. At the same time, carbon dioxide moves from the blood into
    the alveoli so that it can be removed from the body.

    The diaphragm, a large
    muscle located below the lungs, plays an important role in breathing. During
    inhalation, it contracts and moves downward, helping the lungs expand and draw
    air in. During exhalation, it relaxes and moves upward, helping air leave the
    lungs.

    When we exhale, carbon-dioxide-rich air moves from the lungs through
    the bronchi, travels up the trachea, and leaves the body. This cycle of inhalation
    and exhalation continues automatically throughout our lives, providing the oxygen
    our cells need to function.'
